            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for frightened, then the Latin for to panic."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: minae, minārum",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "minae, minārum — \"threats\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Hostēs minās contrā urbem iactābant. The enemy were hurling threats against the city."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Feminine, plural. First the jutting points of a wall, then threats that hang over someone."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "minae first meant \"the jutting points of a wall\" (Lewis & Short); EtymDB traces it to Proto-Italic menā, \"a projection\". English menace comes from its family."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
